In this case then there is some information which can never be known.
The scientist cannot assume in a truly random environment that the game
is even a dungeon game to begin with. The first N levels could have
been generated by a comsmological accident. The majority of the game
could be based on carrot farming.
The player could even assume the game was completely random, but had
some heuregistic to determine fun-ness of a level and regenerate if not
fun. (It just happens think roguelike is the funnest possible)
So.... the player could play 5000->infinity games before realizing that
he never planted enough carrots to win.

<HYPOTHESIS>
If we assume no knowledge of the constants underlying a truly random
system then the answer is:  NO, It can never be _completely_ known.
</HYPOTHESIS>

But can it be known to a statistically significant factor? Phone polls
become statistically significant in the 100s of attempts. But the
difference between 8 and 100 is less than insight gained in the first
eight. Exponentially diminishing returns.

I would assume around 4-10 plays through the entire game could
establish beyond a doubt the types of randomly generated levels. And
probably all the artifacts as well.

